{"subjectId":"c","id":"enums","title":"Enumerations","group":"Structures & User-Defined Types","order":37,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">An <code>enum</code> defines a set of named integer constants — readable names for states, options and error codes.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nenum Weekday { MON, TUE, WED, THU, FRI, SAT, SUN };        /* 0, 1, 2, ... */\nenum Http { OK = 200, CREATED, NOT_FOUND = 404, TEAPOT = 418 };  /* CREATED = 201 */\n\nstatic const char *names[] = {\"Mon\", \"Tue\", \"Wed\", \"Thu\", \"Fri\", \"Sat\", \"Sun\"};\n\nint main(void) {\n    enum Weekday d = FRI;\n    printf(\"%d %s\\n\", d, names[d]);\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\\n\", OK, CREATED, NOT_FOUND);\n    for (enum Weekday w = SAT; w &lt;= SUN; w++) printf(\"%s \", names[w]);\n    printf(\"\\nsizeof(enum Weekday) = %zu\\n\", sizeof(enum Weekday));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>4 Fri\n200 201 404\nSat Sun\nsizeof(enum Weekday) = 4</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-wand\"></i>Idioms</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\n/* a trailing COUNT enumerator gives the number of values for free */\ntypedef enum { RED, GREEN, BLUE, COLOR_COUNT } Color;\n\n/* X-macro: one list generates both the enum and its names */\n#define STATES(X) X(IDLE) X(RUNNING) X(DONE)\n#define AS_ENUM(n) n,\n#define AS_NAME(n) #n,\ntypedef enum { STATES(AS_ENUM) STATE_COUNT } State;\nstatic const char *state_names[] = { STATES(AS_NAME) };\n\nint main(void) {\n    int hits[COLOR_COUNT] = {0};\n    hits[GREEN]++;\n    printf(\"colors: %d, green hits: %d\\n\", COLOR_COUNT, hits[GREEN]);\n    for (int s = 0; s &lt; STATE_COUNT; s++) printf(\"%d=%s \", s, state_names[s]);\n    printf(\"\\n\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>colors: 3, green hits: 1\n0=IDLE 1=RUNNING 2=DONE</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><code>enum</code></th><th><code>#define</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Scoped like variables</td><td>Global text substitution</td></tr><tr><td>Visible in the debugger by name</td><td>Gone after preprocessing</td></tr><tr><td>Auto-numbering</td><td>Every value by hand</td></tr><tr><td><code>switch</code> can warn about missing cases</td><td>No checking</td></tr><tr><td>Only <code>int</code> values (C23 allows a fixed underlying type)</td><td>Any value/type (strings, doubles)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>C enums are <em>weakly</em> typed: an <code>enum</code> variable is just an integer, and any <code>int</code> can be assigned to it without a diagnostic. Enumerator names share one namespace — prefix them (<code>COLOR_RED</code>) to avoid clashes.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the default value of the first enumerator, and of the ones after it?</summary><div><p>The first is 0 unless given a value; each subsequent enumerator is the previous value + 1. Values can repeat and can be negative.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the size of an enum?</summary><div><p>Implementation-defined: an integer type able to represent all enumerators — <code>int</code> (4 bytes) on common compilers. C23 lets you fix it: <code>enum Small : unsigned char { A, B };</code>.</p></div></details></section>"}